          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.6 Food Miles: From Farm to Our Plate"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.121,
            0.884,
            0.248
          ],
          "content": "How does food reach from a farm to our plate? What are the steps involved in this process? Who are the people involved in this process? Do you know how much time and effort is required to get the wheat flour once seed grains germinate in the farm? Let us look at Fig. 3.10 to understand the entire process of making the chapati that we eat."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.262,
            0.28,
            0.877,
            0.678
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.452,
                0.682,
                0.687,
                0.701
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.10: From farm to plate"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_19_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.723,
            0.885,
            0.85
          ],
          "content": "The entire distance travelled by a bag of wheat or any other food item, from the producer to the consumer, is known as its **food miles**. Reducing food miles is important because it helps to cut down the cost and pollution during its transport; it helps support local farmers; and it also keeps our food fresh and healthy."
        },
